1
00:00:02,402 --> 00:00:06,567
[Subtitles by danielsangeo]

2
00:00:07,434 --> 00:00:08,968
"You're in the barrel."

3
00:00:10,192 --> 00:00:13,033
Oh, wait. I can't
remember which way to go.

4
00:00:13,903 --> 00:00:16,133
Was it left? Right?

5
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:19,498
Nnnnnnnrrryuhh...
I'll go this way.

6
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:20,500
Hope it's good.

7
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,033
Safety!

8
00:00:24,801 --> 00:00:27,237
American bad ass!

9
00:00:27,237 --> 00:00:30,176
And more soulless walls of concrete.

10
00:00:30,176 --> 00:00:33,500
Wait a second. Was that the same hallway?

11
00:00:34,438 --> 00:00:37,048
Ohh! It is!

12
00:00:37,048 --> 00:00:39,246
It didn't even matter.

13
00:00:39,246 --> 00:00:42,272
Those sneaky architect bastards.

14
00:00:42,272 --> 00:00:46,721
I have to say, though, working in an
underground laboratory is pretty cool.

15
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:49,667
It kind of makes me feel like
I'm an evil scientist.

16
00:00:50,568 --> 00:00:53,434
I always wanted to be an evil scientist.

17
00:00:54,536 --> 00:00:56,100
Muhahahahahaha...

18
00:00:56,607 --> 00:00:58,200
Uh-hahahahahahaha...

19
00:00:58,907 --> 00:01:00,467
Huhehehahahahahaha...!

20
00:01:01,032 --> 00:01:02,777
WAAH-HAH-HAH-HAH-HAH-Hah!

21
00:01:02,777 --> 00:01:04,014
Okay, I'd better chill out.

22
00:01:04,014 --> 00:01:05,634
I don't want a repeat of Monday.

23
00:01:07,177 --> 00:01:08,344
Deep breaths...

24
00:01:08,344 --> 00:01:09,341
SCIENTIST: Greetings.

25
00:01:09,341 --> 00:01:12,899
Heeeeyyyyy... 
wait, I don't know you.

26
00:01:12,899 --> 00:01:14,100
Don't confuse me.

27
00:01:17,629 --> 00:01:20,367
Everything... this way. Awesome.

28
00:01:23,436 --> 00:01:24,968
LASER!

29
00:01:24,968 --> 00:01:25,544
Caution.

30
00:01:25,544 --> 00:01:27,223
LASER!

31
00:01:27,223 --> 00:01:27,795
Caution.

32
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:29,461
LASER!

33
00:01:29,461 --> 00:01:29,984
Caution.

34
00:01:29,984 --> 00:01:31,696
LASER!

35
00:01:31,696 --> 00:01:33,334
What could it mean?

36
00:01:36,243 --> 00:01:37,719
How about you? You know any jokes?

37
00:01:37,719 --> 00:01:38,204
No?

38
00:01:38,204 --> 00:01:38,767
Oh well.

39
00:01:42,397 --> 00:01:44,400
Man, I've gotta get
my mind out of the gutter.

40
00:01:45,349 --> 00:01:48,211
Okay, could this hallway
be more depressing?

41
00:01:48,211 --> 00:01:50,209
This lighting is crap!

42
00:01:50,209 --> 00:01:53,691
It's either too dark or has this
sickly green look.

43
00:01:53,691 --> 00:01:57,101
Y'know, it wouldn't kill them to get some
bulbs down here that give off UV rays.

44
00:01:57,101 --> 00:01:58,704
SCIENTIST #1: Ah, Gordon, here you are.

45
00:01:58,704 --> 00:02:01,005
SCIENTIST #1: We just sent the
sample down to the test chamber.

46
00:02:01,005 --> 00:02:04,319
SCIENTIST #2: We've boosted the
Anti-Mass Spectrometer to 105%.

47
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:05,048
Nice hair.

48
00:02:05,048 --> 00:02:07,480
SCIENTIST #2: Bit of a gamble,
but we need the extra resolution.

49
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:10,058
SCIENTIST #3: The administrator
is very concerned that we get a

50
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:12,579
SCIENTIST #3: conclusive
analysis of today's sample.

51
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:14,776
SCIENTIST #3: I gather they went
to some lengths to get it.

52
00:02:14,776 --> 00:02:16,236
FedEx'd it and everything, huh?

53
00:02:16,236 --> 00:02:20,062
SCIENTIST #1: They're waiting
for you, Gordon, in the test chamber.

54
00:02:20,062 --> 00:02:24,167
You know, sometimes I think you
guys are just robots, except not cool.

55
00:02:25,879 --> 00:02:29,634
I wonder if we built this facility
on an ancient Indian burial ground.

56
00:02:30,478 --> 00:02:32,367
I mean, this is New Mexico.

57
00:02:34,691 --> 00:02:37,601
You know, I still have no idea
what these things are.

58
00:02:38,044 --> 00:02:39,805
SCIENTIST #4: Ah, it's good to see you.

59
00:02:39,805 --> 00:02:42,239
Yes, it IS good to see me.

60
00:02:42,239 --> 00:02:46,297
They look like toy plasma globes,
but what use would we have for that?

61
00:02:46,297 --> 00:02:47,389
[shattering sound]
HIT THE DECK!

62
00:02:47,389 --> 00:02:48,972
SCIENTIST #5: It's about to go critical!

63
00:02:48,972 --> 00:02:50,375
I thought it was a sniper...!

64
00:02:50,375 --> 00:02:53,106
SCIENTIST #4: What the hell is
going on with our equipment?

65
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:55,134
SCIENTIST #5: It wasn't meant to do this
I guess that wouldn't make sense.

66
00:02:55,134 --> 00:02:56,063
SCIENTIST #5: in the first place!

67
00:02:56,063 --> 00:02:57,267
Let's have a look here.

68
00:02:57,853 --> 00:02:59,761
Ah, yeah, that's busted.

69
00:02:59,761 --> 00:03:00,326
[shattering sound]

70
00:03:00,326 --> 00:03:00,734
Whoa!

71
00:03:01,912 --> 00:03:04,408
Damned Cyrix processors...

72
00:03:04,408 --> 00:03:06,901
You go with the lowest bidder
and this is what happens.

73
00:03:07,867 --> 00:03:09,234
Glad that's not my problem.

74
00:03:10,783 --> 00:03:13,261
Oh no. Not this elevator.

75
00:03:13,261 --> 00:03:15,234
I hate it; it always makes me dizzy.

76
00:03:15,805 --> 00:03:19,261
Okay, maybe if I turn at the
right time with it, I won't be dizzy.

77
00:03:19,261 --> 00:03:21,909
Left... right... no, wait, the other way.

78
00:03:21,909 --> 00:03:23,268
Clockwise...

79
00:03:23,268 --> 00:03:25,100
Counter-clockwise... no!

80
00:03:25,819 --> 00:03:28,400
Oh man, I still messed it up
again; I'm still dizzy.

81
00:03:29,017 --> 00:03:32,570
Well, it's not as bad as those
teacup rides at Disneyland.

82
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:35,394
I hate those. They're designed
so if you puke out the side,

83
00:03:35,394 --> 00:03:37,691
you spray as many people as possible.

84
00:03:37,691 --> 00:03:38,252
SCIENTIST: Freeman.

85
00:03:38,252 --> 00:03:39,033
You know it!

86
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:40,133
Hm.

87
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:43,334
Am I the only person here with a beard?

88
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:46,968
I understand that the guards
don't have them, but the scientists?

89
00:03:47,742 --> 00:03:49,465
Something's wrong here.

90
00:03:49,465 --> 00:03:52,003
SCIENTIST #6: I'm afraid we'll
be deviating a bit

91
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:54,735
SCIENTIST #6: from Standard
Analysis Procedures today, Gordon.

92
00:03:54,735 --> 00:03:55,456
I don't care.

93
00:03:55,456 --> 00:03:57,819
SCIENTIST #7: Yes, but with good reason.

94
00:03:57,819 --> 00:03:59,889
SCIENTIST #7: This is a rare
opportunity for us.

95
00:03:59,889 --> 00:04:02,374
SCIENTIST #7: This is the purest
sample we've seen yet.

96
00:04:02,374 --> 00:04:04,967
SCIENTIST #6: And, potentially,
the most unstable.

97
00:04:04,967 --> 00:04:08,069
SCIENTIST #7: Now now, if you'll
follow Standard Insertion Procedures,

98
00:04:08,069 --> 00:04:09,543
SCIENTIST #7: everything will be fine.

99
00:04:09,543 --> 00:04:11,269
SCIENTIST #6: I don't know how
you can say that,

100
00:04:11,269 --> 00:04:13,406
SCIENTIST #6: although I will
admit that the possibility of a

101
00:04:13,406 --> 00:04:16,461
SCIENTIST #6: Resonance Cascade
Scenario is extremely unlikely--

102
00:04:16,461 --> 00:04:20,612
SCIENTIST #7: Gordon doesn't need to hear
all this; he's a highly-trained professional!

103
00:04:20,612 --> 00:04:21,391
Hell yeah!

104
00:04:21,391 --> 00:04:24,760
SCIENTIST #7: We've assured the
administrator that nothing will go wrong?

105
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:27,139
SCIENTIST #6: Ah, yes, you're right.

106
00:04:27,139 --> 00:04:29,487
SCIENTIST #6: Gordon, we have
complete confidence in you.

107
00:04:29,487 --> 00:04:30,160
Me too!

108
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:32,186
SCIENTIST #7: Well, go ahead.
Let's let him in now.

109
00:04:32,186 --> 00:04:33,810
Does my beard intimidate you?

110
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,801
[rapid beeping]
Nyaaaaahhh! Stop that noise!

111
00:04:37,537 --> 00:04:38,901
I hate that!

112
00:04:39,387 --> 00:04:43,959
It reminds me of that dream I have where I'm
strapped to a gurney watching Fraggle Rock

113
00:04:43,959 --> 00:04:46,409
with flashing lights on either side of me.

114
00:04:46,409 --> 00:04:48,801
Then I realize: I'm in Hell!

115
00:04:49,630 --> 00:04:51,488
It's all crap anyway.

116
00:04:51,488 --> 00:04:53,414
The only reason we have those scanners

117
00:04:53,414 --> 00:04:56,598
is because they caught playing
racquetball in here once.

118
00:04:56,598 --> 00:04:57,370
[microphone feedback, Gordon prepares to hock a loogie]

119
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:59,167
SCIENTIST #8: Testing, testing...
Eh, better not.

120
00:05:01,024 --> 00:05:02,934
SCIENTIST #8: Everything seems
to be in order.

121
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,190
Roger, Roger. What's your vector, Victor?

122
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:10,217
SCIENTIST #9: All right, Gordon, your suit should
keep you comfortable through all this.

123
00:05:10,217 --> 00:05:13,227
SCIENTIST #9: The specimen will
be delivered to you in a few moments.

124
00:05:13,227 --> 00:05:17,518
SCIENTIST #9: If you would be so good
as to climb up and start the rotors,

125
00:05:17,518 --> 00:05:18,042
Oh, yeah.

126
00:05:18,042 --> 00:05:20,891
SCIENTIST #9: we can bring the
Anti-Mass Spectrometer to 80%

127
00:05:20,891 --> 00:05:24,705
SCIENTIST #9: and hold it there
until the carrier arrives.

128
00:05:24,705 --> 00:05:26,345
I like climbing things.

129
00:05:26,345 --> 00:05:28,932
It appeals to my simian instincts.

130
00:05:28,932 --> 00:05:32,050
And also makes me regret how I
have no tail.

131
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:36,231
Damn my genes. I have no tail
and I must swing.

132
00:05:36,231 --> 00:05:40,532
SCIENTIST #9: Gordon, are you not hearing
me? Climb up and start the rotors, please.

133
00:05:40,532 --> 00:05:42,781
Wha-what do you think I'm doing?

134
00:05:42,781 --> 00:05:45,955
Hey, I could just sit here and
not do a damned thing.

135
00:05:45,955 --> 00:05:48,101
Bet you'd like that, huh?

136
00:05:48,101 --> 00:05:50,070
Ungrateful pricks.

137
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:52,888
I'm going to start making a list
of names around here.

138
00:05:52,888 --> 00:05:55,230
And you're going to be towards
the top of the list.

139
00:05:55,230 --> 00:05:56,996
This is getting ridiculous.

140
00:05:56,996 --> 00:06:01,059
I never thought I'd have so many enemies
that I'd need to make an actual list.

141
00:06:01,059 --> 00:06:04,152
But, it's just getting to be
more than I can keep track of.

142
00:06:04,152 --> 00:06:07,286
I mean, what the hell, it worked
for Nixon, it should work for me, right?

143
00:06:07,286 --> 00:06:08,797
SCIENTIST #9: Very good.

144
00:06:08,797 --> 00:06:10,183
SCIENTIST #9: We'll take it from here.

145
00:06:10,183 --> 00:06:16,662
SCIENTIST #8: Power to Stage I Emitters
in 3... 2... 1...

146
00:06:16,662 --> 00:06:18,224
Pow!

147
00:06:18,224 --> 00:06:21,321
SCIENTIST #8: I'm seeing
predictable phase arrays.

148
00:06:21,321 --> 00:06:21,734
Yeah.

149
00:06:22,559 --> 00:06:25,734
At times like this I remember
why I became a physicist:

150
00:06:25,734 --> 00:06:29,407
To show antimatter particles
who's BOSS! YEAH!

151
00:06:29,407 --> 00:06:33,259
SCIENTIST #8: Stage II Emitters
activating...now.

152
00:06:33,259 --> 00:06:34,329
Pew!

153
00:06:34,329 --> 00:06:36,339
Awesome. Totally awesome.

154
00:06:36,339 --> 00:06:40,493
SCIENTIST #9: Gordon, we cannot predict how
long the system can operate at this level,

155
00:06:40,493 --> 00:06:43,978
SCIENTIST #9: nor how long the reading will
take. Please, work as quickly as you can.

156
00:06:43,978 --> 00:06:46,664
I'm not talking to you. You're on the list.

157
00:06:46,664 --> 00:06:48,268
I'm doing this because I want to.

158
00:06:48,268 --> 00:06:52,562
SCIENTIST #8: Overhead capacitors
to one-oh-five percent.

159
00:06:52,562 --> 00:06:54,273
Wait, isn't that a little dangerous?

160
00:06:54,273 --> 00:06:57,704
SCIENTIST #8: Uhh... it's probably
not a problem... probably.

161
00:06:57,704 --> 00:07:00,937
SCIENTIST #8: But I'm showing
small discrepancy in... well,

162
00:07:00,937 --> 00:07:01,321
What?

163
00:07:01,321 --> 00:07:03,897
SCIENTIST #8: no, it's well
within acceptable bounds again.

164
00:07:03,897 --> 00:07:04,961
SCIENTIST #8: Sustaining sequence.

165
00:07:04,961 --> 00:07:06,217
Man, don't jinx us.

166
00:07:06,217 --> 00:07:10,103
SCIENTIST #9: I've just been
informed that the sample is ready, Gordon.

167
00:07:10,103 --> 00:07:13,312
SCIENTIST #9: It should be
coming up to you any moment now.

168
00:07:13,312 --> 00:07:16,544
SCIENTIST #9: Look to the
delivery system for your specimen.

169
00:07:16,544 --> 00:07:19,239
Seriously, I hope we get some
good readings today.

170
00:07:19,239 --> 00:07:22,834
Depending on what we find, we
might just disprove string theory.

171
00:07:22,834 --> 00:07:24,746
That would make my day.

172
00:07:24,746 --> 00:07:27,042
If we do, I'm not going to be
gracious about it, either.

173
00:07:27,042 --> 00:07:29,334
I'm going to rub people's FACES in it.

174
00:07:30,688 --> 00:07:33,413
So, what's the deal, guys?
You ready or what?

175
00:07:33,413 --> 00:07:36,313
SCIENTIST #9: Standard insertion
for a nonstandard specimen.

176
00:07:36,313 --> 00:07:36,938
All right.

177
00:07:36,938 --> 00:07:40,229
SCIENTIST #9: Go ahead, Gordon,
schlomp the carrier into the analysis port.

178
00:07:40,229 --> 00:07:41,720
I hate it when he talks like that.

179
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,658
Next, he's going to start
smacking his lips.

180
00:07:43,658 --> 00:07:45,003
SCIENTIST #10: What is he doing in there?

181
00:07:45,003 --> 00:07:45,361
Huh?

182
00:07:45,361 --> 00:07:48,924
SCIENTIST #9: Nothing you need to worry
about, Gordon. Heh. That's it, uh--

183
00:07:48,924 --> 00:07:49,768
SCIENTIST #9: Go ahead.

184
00:07:49,768 --> 00:07:53,095
Huh, yeah, wouldn't want to take
the blinders off the horse, now would ya?

185
00:07:53,095 --> 00:07:54,012
Whatever.

186
00:07:54,012 --> 00:07:55,866
[high-pitched whine, teleportation sounds]
OH GOD!

187
00:07:55,866 --> 00:07:56,849
WHAT-WHAT DO I DO? WHAT-WHAT DO I DO?

188
00:07:56,849 --> 00:07:57,985
SCIENTIST #9: GORDON, GET AWAY
FROM THE BEAM!

189
00:07:57,985 --> 00:07:58,901
SCIENTIST #8: SHUTTING DOWN...!

190
00:07:58,901 --> 00:08:01,645
OH, NO, NO, NO! THIS IS NOT GOOD!

191
00:08:01,645 --> 00:08:02,423
SCIENTIST #8: IT'S NOT...!

192
00:08:02,423 --> 00:08:03,308
LET ME OUT OF HERE!

193
00:08:03,308 --> 00:08:06,392
SCIENTIST #8: IT'S NOT...IT'S NOT SHUTTING DOWN!
OPEN UP THE DOOR!

194
00:08:06,392 --> 00:08:08,801
[scientists scream, explosions]
OPEN THE DOOR, YOU BASTARDS!

195
00:08:09,670 --> 00:08:11,858
RRRRGH! I HATE YOU!

196
00:08:11,858 --> 00:08:14,334
THIS IS A BAD EXPERIMENT!

197
00:08:14,887 --> 00:08:17,200
WE ARE BAD PEOPLE!

198
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:21,434
WHY DID WE USHER FORTH
THE GREEN APOCALYPSE?!

199
00:08:22,515 --> 00:08:23,367
What happened?!

200
00:08:23,851 --> 00:08:24,567
What is this?

201
00:08:25,728 --> 00:08:26,534
Am I dead?

202
00:08:27,582 --> 00:08:28,734
I don't feel dead.

203
00:08:29,418 --> 00:08:30,467
But, how would I know?

204
00:08:31,588 --> 00:08:35,467
If this is what it's like to be
dead, then being dead SUCKS!

205
00:08:37,112 --> 00:08:39,465
Oh, shit! That's the ceiling!

206
00:08:39,465 --> 00:08:40,767
WHERE'S MY HELMET?!

207
00:08:43,792 --> 00:08:45,667
...what the fuck?

208
00:08:48,987 --> 00:08:50,133
WHO ARE YOU?!

209
00:08:51,893 --> 00:08:52,701
NO!

210
00:08:53,548 --> 00:08:56,033
I DON'T WANNA BE A SCHIZOPHRENIC!

211
00:08:57,510 --> 00:09:03,000
Oh my God, this is crazy-in-a-box
with a side order of fries!

